INT. PAWNSHOP - BACK ROOM - NIGHT

Shelves of guitars, watches, and wedding rings under a single hanging bulb. A poker table with a half-played hand still on it.

MORDECAI "MOE" LASALLE, 50s, pawnbroker, shuffles a deck, humming. Across from him, a young woman, ZOFIA KRAWCZYK, 20s, drums her fingers.

                    MOE
Zofia. Sweetheart. You came alone.

                    ZOFIA
Like you said.

                    MOE
Good girl.

                    ZOFIA
I'm not your girl, Moe.

                    MOE
Everyone's somebody's girl.

He deals. She doesn't touch her cards.

                    MOE (CONT'D)
(to the empty room)
Anyone else want in?

Silence. She smiles, thin.

                    ZOFIA
You think I'm stupid.

                    MOE
I think you're tired.

                    ZOFIA
I'm tired of you, Moe.

She reaches under the table. Moe's shuffling stops.

                    MOE
(muttering)
Oh, don't.

Zofia lays a small recorder on the felt. Presses play.

                    RECORDING (V.O.)
...and I told him, if the Kessler job goes wrong, it's on Zofia.

Moe stares at the recorder. Then, very slowly, he laughs.

                    MOE
Smart girl.
